    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-988",
      "problem": "Calculate the slope of the line shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14166993/assets/48114216e210a4e4dfc353871bbfd16bd2beb480f5831c62189db9299e09f517.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the line passes through the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, we let $(x_1,y_1) = (-2,0)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (0,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-0}{0-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=1.$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14166993/observations/step-e988-d967bcadb8522a807608.html",
